<p>
Almost every Irreal reader, I’m sure, knows about the Technical Capability Notice that the UK government issued to Apple back in 2025 ordering them to provide a backdoor to encrypted iCloud data. Part of law authorizing TCNs (the Investigatory Powers Act) also mandates that the recipient can’t talk about them or even admit to receiving one.
</p>
<p>
“Somehow” the Washington Post found out about the TCN and wrote about it. Response in the U.S. was swift and virulent. Even the President weighed in with only slightly veiled threats of the consequences if the TCN weren’t withdrawn.
</p>
<p>
Apple, for its part, never mentioned the TCN and declined all comment on it but they did withdraw the encrypted iCloud facility (Advanced Data Protection) from UK users. It was, in short, an open secret that the UK had issued a TCN but the UK government refused to acknowledge the fact or comment on it in any way. Apple <i>did</i> say that they would not under any circumstances provide a backdoor into any of their systems but they didn’t tie this to the TCN.
</p>
<p>
In the end, the UK did withdraw the TCN but issued another more closely drawn one that applied only to UK citizens but they still refused to admit to the TCN or discuss it at all. Unfortunately for the UK spooks, the law also provides for a Investigatory Powers Tribunal that can hear appeals to actions under the Investigatory Powers Act, and <a href="https://macanorak.com/uk-government-forced-to-admit-it-issued-apple-a-secret-encryption-order/">the tribunal has just ordered that Apple can talk about what everybody already know is true</a>.
</p>
<p>
Truthfully, I’m not sure that this will make any difference in the end. The spooks will still try to spy on us as much as they can. My takeaway is that say what you will about Apple, they’re unequivocal about their position: No back doors. Ever.</p>
